  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nUpper trough over the Great Lakes region will keep northeast\nWisconsin in a cyclonic flow pattern through Friday, leading to a\nstretch of cooler and cloudy conditions, with on and off batches\nof showers--rain and snow.\n\nSurface cold front currently stretches from about Oconto back\nthrough Clintonville and Stevens Point, with the next round of\nlight showers coming in along and behind the front. This batch\nwill move south this afternoon, with light rain accumulations\n(less than 0.10\") along it. Behind the front, embedded shortwaves\nalong with cold north winds over Lake Superior and Lake Michigan\nwill generate more showers tonight and into Thursday, with some \ncontinued presence into Friday as well. Exact timing is difficult\nto pin down, as precip will generally be light and short-lived at\nany one location, but the potential exists throughout due to the \nlake- enhancement and general cold, cyclonic flow. Probabilities \nfor measurable rain range from 50-100% from now through Friday \nmorning, but drop to near 0% for at least 0.25\" of rain outside of\nthe common lake effect areas--this includes far north central WI \n(mainly Vilas County) and counties along Lake Michigan. \nProbabilities of a quarter inch range from 30-50% in these areas \ndue to their proximity to lake bands and more persistent precip at\ntimes.\n\nP-types remain rain this afternoon, with a changeover to \nsnow tonight, particularly across central and northern northern \nWI. While up to 1-2\" of snow is forecast to fall in Vilas County, \nmeasurable accumulation may be limited by warmer ground and near- \nsurface temps, especially during the day. This could lead to some \nbrief, minor vsby reductions, but overall expect impacts to remain\nminimal. Across the Fox Valley and lakeshore, any snowflakes are \nlikely to be brief and/or mixed with rain. Door County is the \nexception as there are some indications a narrow band of snow \ncould form here Thursday night. But right now probabilities for 1\"\nof snow or more in Door County are negligible. Kept mention of \nsprinkles/flurries through Thursday for all areas where PoPs were \nbelow 15. \n\nA final shortwave comes through Saturday which keeps minor PoPs \nin the east, then upper ridge and surface high arrive on Sunday. \nExpect dry weather and light winds through early next week, along \nwith a warming temperature trend. High temperatures will be in \nthe 40s to low 50s, ~5 degrees warmer than normal.",
